From b95d7457f730bd036852095360235df56620d61c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NathanNeurotic <109461996+NathanNeurotic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:40:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] smbman: send at most 65534 bytes per WRITE_ANDX request

smb_WriteAndX() sets the request's 16-bit ByteCount to the data length
plus its one padding byte, and smb_WriteFile() sent up to
CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE (65535) bytes per request, so a 65535-byte request
went out with a ByteCount of 0. IOP callers that write 65535 bytes or
more in one call reach it; EE fileXio callers only after raising the RW
buffer to at least that size.

Send at most CLIENT_MAX_WRITE_SIZE (65534) bytes per request. Reads keep
CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE. How servers treat a ByteCount of 0 was not
checked.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RwNwRWSWvoz4YfK5AEj37D
---
 iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c b/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
index efb3ae306..fb109e9ec 100644
--- a/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
+++ b/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@ static server_specs_t server_specs;
 
 #define CLIENT_MAX_BUFFER_SIZE USHRT_MAX // Allow up to 65535 bytes to be received.
 #define CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE   USHRT_MAX // Allow up to 65535 bytes to be transferred.
+// A WRITE_ANDX request's 16-bit ByteCount covers its padding byte and the data.
+#define CLIENT_MAX_WRITE_SIZE  (CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE - 1)
 
 static int main_socket = -1;
 
@@ -1123,7 +1125,7 @@ int smb_WriteAndX(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int
     // report a full disk with an error status (caught above) or with success and a Count of zero,
     // and a server may store less than it was sent. So return the server's Count: smb_WriteFile()
     // sends the rest from there, and on a zero or a failure returns what earlier requests stored.
-    // It asks for at most CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE (65535) bytes, so the 16-bit Count holds the whole
+    // It asks for at most CLIENT_MAX_WRITE_SIZE (65534) bytes, so the 16-bit Count holds the whole
     // answer and CountHigh is not read. A reply shorter than the six-word response (45 bytes), a
     // WordCount under 6, or a Count over what was sent is a failed write.
     if (r < (int)sizeof(WriteAndXResponse_t) || WRsp->smbWordcount < 6 || WRsp->Count > nbytes)
@@ -1145,7 +1147,7 @@ int smb_WriteFile(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int
     while (remaining > 0) {
         int result, toWrite;
 
-        toWrite = remaining > CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE ? CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE : remaining;
+        toWrite = remaining > CLIENT_MAX_WRITE_SIZE ? CLIENT_MAX_WRITE_SIZE : remaining;
 
         result = smb_WriteAndX(UID, TID, FID, pos, ptr, toWrite);
         if (result <= 0) // the server stored what earlier requests sent, and no more
